\f1\fs22 Sarah knocked on the sliding glass door at the back of Chip Stockwell\'92s Sublime Point townhouse. When she was met with a protracting silence, she put her hand against the glass to shield against glare and peered. She saw no signs of life, but noticed the eating area and kitchen were a mirror-image of her townhouse, except for a wooden screen leant up against the stasis locker door that depicted a scene of skinny women and sumo wrestlers in acts of coitus in a panorama of snow-capped mountains.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah wrinkled her nose and banged on the glass door. \'91Mr Stockwell!\'92 A few more bangs. \'91Mr Stockwell. The barbecue has already started. I\'92m surprised you can\'92t smell it \'85 I mean there\'92s been a lot of smoke for a couple of hours now.\'92 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah tried one more round of banging, then stepped back and surveyed the the first floor windows, wondering which bedroom Chip might be using. She\'92s assumed he\'92d use the main bedroom suite, but similar banging on the front door had yielded no results either. She considered calling security, pretty sure Eloise would back her up if she had a door broken down and Chip dragged howling to the barbecue next door.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The glass door slid open with fury. \'91What?\'92 its animator demanded.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The stood Chip puffy from sleep wearing a bad case of bed head and three-day growth, clad only in a dilapidated white terry towelling robe the almost but not quite failed to conceal a pink hairy chest and a pair of baggy white underpants whose loose waistband elastic had retired from duty many years ago and was threatening to let the whole side down at a moment\'92s notice. Sarah stared at the Sumo dudes on the screen and somehow found them less objectionable.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You\'92re late,\'92 Sarah stated, still looking anywhere but at Chip.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I\'92m not coming. Good morning.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You already agreed. You told me you would come. Eloise told you to tell me you would come.\'92 Sarah lifted her eyebrows while they said, 
\f2\i And no one refuses Eloise Shakti
\f1\i0 .\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip pondered what to do, and chose aggression. \'91Don\'92t toy with me, girlie,\'92 he threatened.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Don\'92t toy with Eloise,\'92 she answered, fearing his loose underwear were only one tired elastic snap from revealing the paucity of 
\f2\i his
\f1\i0  toys. Sarah gulped. \'91Besides, if those who have suffered greatly at your hand can offer you their peace and compassion, how can you obstinately remain the lesser man?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip sighed. He\'92d genuinely hoped the invitation was simply a social formality. How could the Chuglinghams want him in their home after all he\'92d perpetrated on them? How could they even stand to live next door to him? Although the kids 
\f2\i did
\f1\i0  throw lit fireworks into his gutters at all hours of the night. More than once, they\'92d concussed possums whose subsequent deranged scratchings at the roof to avoid falling off were more irritating than the fireworks.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Fine,\'92 he conceded. \'91I\'92ll be there in ten minutes.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I\'92ll wait for you.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip stared at Sarah until he was sure she wasn\'92t kidding, that she wasn\'92t going to let him off. \'91I\'92ll just put some pants on.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91That would be a great leap forward in neighbourhood diplomacy.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip vanished and returned seven or eight minutes later.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You could at least pretend to be looking forward to this,\'92 Sarah said as they walked to the back yard gate.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I 
\f2\i am
\f1\i0  pretending,; Chip replied. \'91Pretending I don\'92t know them, I'm pretending I didn't sign off on sending giving them a never-ending holiday in off-shore detention. I'm pretending this isn't going to be the most excruciating social event since public executions went invitation-only.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Well, the Chuglinghams have put all that behind them, so I\'92m glad that you can too.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chips jaw went slack.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah smiled. \'91Good. Now be a gracious guest and eat whatever overpriced protein Mr. Chuglingham inevitably cremates on his new ten-thousand-dollar barbecue.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91One hour,\'92 Chip said as they went though the gate. \'91Then I'm developing a sudden and severe migraine.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Two hours,\'92 Sarah countered. \'91And you have to eat something.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>They flooded the circuitous path that arced away from the townhouse, between wattle and under eucalypts. To their left, ochre cliffs and sharp blue sky met the Sublime Point cliff and peered at them through the trees. The view was objectively spectacular\'97the kind that the Airborne paid obscene amounts to possess and Cloudborne only saw in advertisements for things they couldn't afford. Chip gave no impression he appreciated any of it. Eventually the path delivered them to the Chuglingham\'92s back gate, no more than twenty metres from where they\'92d left Chip\'92s yard.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Wearing an apron that still had the fold lines from its packaging, Balasubramaniam Chuglingham operated the control panel of a smoke-billowing apparatus that resembled a barbecue in exactly the same way that a sparkling new, yet sinking, steamship might. Its stainless steel surface reflected the afternoon sun with almost vindictive brightness.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He adjusted dials with all the acumen of a parrot at submarine weapons console. He lifted the lid and peered. He moved steaks and sausages around the grill like they were two sets of pieces in a version of lightning chess that required extra speed because all the pieces were on fire. He put the lid down. He dialled and twiddled, and lifted the lid again. Clearly, he had never cooked outdoors, before the Layer had been removed from above his head. The ever-increasing haze of smoke billowing around him carried the aroma of expensive meat suffering cremation. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>At the sound of the back gate creaking open and shutting he looked up from his tribulations. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Welcome, welcome!\'92 he called out, waving a pair of tongs like a conductor's baton. \'91Sarah! And... Mr. Stockwell, isn't it?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip have a curt nod of Chuglingham\'92s pretence of meeting for the first time. Then their eyes met in a moment of mutual recognition that Sarah, mercifully, didn't notice.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Ah, yes \'85 ah Bama Super Cali \'85?\'92 Chip felt foolish.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Oh, I have an impossibly long name, is it? That\'92s ok, you can call me Balasubra.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip blinked and Mr Chuglingham laughed a genial laugh. \'91Bala is just fine, Mr Stockwell.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Chip is just fine Mr Bala.\'92 Chip\'92s prodigious social skills always managed to desert him at times when social skills were not only required, but often life-preserving. Chip was still not sure which of those occasions this barbecue would be classified as.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Are you having a beer?\'92 Bala asked Chip now they were on an abbreviated first name basis, and gestured at an esky in the shadows under the patios awning. It looked big enough to chill a whole deer, or possibly moose, for days.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Bala, careful with the temperature!\'92 Mrs Padmalakshmipriyamvada Chuglingham hurried across the terrace, a platter of raw meat in her hands. She was dressed in what Airborne catalogs would call "casual luxury"\'97clothes designed to look effortless while costing more than most Cloudborne crippling tax bills.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I have it under control,\'92 Bala assured her, pressing a button that caused flames to leap two feet high. \'91Just adding some \'85 dramatic flair. You see it is under control, Padmala.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91But it is not under control, Bala,\'92 Padmala complained, frankly out of sorts at being relieved of cooking duties.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Perhaps I could help, young Bala.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The three sages had arrived silently and unnoticed, each dressed in casual wizard hats, light open robes, board shorts and sandals, and carrying easies of their own.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Oh, Mr Kleo, I could not possibly \'85\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleo let the half-uttered objection hand in the air a while then said, \'91I would be my honour. After all, one doesn\'92t simply become one of the Seven Sages without mastering the elements.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>All but Shilo and Chilo waited blankly for more explanation.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91The four elements, one of which is fire, young Bala,\'92 Kleo explained.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It means, we can cook,\'92 Shilo explained as Kleo relieved Bala of the barbecue tongs.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Oh, thank Murugan.\'92 Bala stepped back, removed his apron and offered it to Kleo.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Thank you, young Bala, but I\'92ll manage in my own ceremonial barbecuing robes.\'92 Kleo set his esky down, opened it and fossicked about as he looked up at Bala and winked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A child's wail pierced the afternoon calm. A boy of about seven had tripped on the garden path, his knee scraped and   small about of blood oozing. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Paddy set down the platter and hurried to her son. \'91Tiki, what happened?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I was looking at the birds,\'92 the boy sniffled, pointing skyward. \'91The real ones.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Fifth time today," Bala explained, abandoning his post at the grill. \'91They can't stop looking up. Never saw an open sky before we moved here.\'92 Bala lifted his son to his feet while Padmala licked her fingers and dabbed at his wound.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Come meet our guests,\'92 Bala said and led his child for presentation.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alongside, Padmala said, \'91This is our son Tikirimanikandarajah. Say hello, Tiki.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Hello.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Among the counter greetings Shilo knelt before Tiki and examined his wound. \'91You know, Tikirimanikandarajah,\'92 he said playfully, while pronouncing the boys name perfectly. \'91I once scraped my knee during the capture of Salamis, and I\'92m still alive to tell the tale.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tiki giggled.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91And it\'92s worth it, to appreciate the beauty of the birds and sky, isn\'92t it?\'92 Shilo asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The boy nodded, a little embarrassed to be receiving such attention.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91There\'92s a good lad.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>While Shilo amused Tiki, Chilo extracted a bottle of retsina from his esky ands corked it, while Kleo made room on the wide stainless steel grill, shifting Bala\'92s tortured meats to cooler parts.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Don\'92t be alarmed,\'92 Kleo told Bala. \'91We can rescue those. They won\'92t have died for nothing.\'92 Bala didn\'92t notice Kleo\'92s boggling eyes communicating the exact opposite.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Having made room, Kleo pulled a large octopus and a larger butchered haunch and a from his esky, which he threw on the barbecue with such vigour the grill panels shook.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Bala regarded his steaks and sausages and sighed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Misconstruing Bala\'92s thoughts, Kleo said, \'91Well, I would have brought a live goat but Sarah assured me you don\'92t celebrate quite the same way around here.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>As if to confirm Sarah\'92s counsel, Tiki ran to the barbecue with grief breaking out across his. \'91Are they cooking a baby lamb, Amma?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chilo regarded the boy with a condescending scowl. \'91It\'92s kid, not lamb,\'92 he corrected.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tiki wailed and ran for his life. \'91They are cooking kids, Amma!\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Oh, hush and don\'92t be such a child,\'92 Chilo called after Tiki. \'91In my day you\'92d get nothing but scraps after the soldiers had eaten, and that would be after a long day of collecting abandoned weapons and slaying wounded survivors on the battlefield.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleo took Chilo\'92s arm and spoke to his ear. \'91Keep your head down, Chilo. Sometimes I wonder if you forget what century this is.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I know exactly what century it is,\'92 Chilo asserted. \'91The last one.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The long patio table had transformed into a culinary United Nations. With the meats cooked and delivered to the table on the patio, Hayley and Padmala laid plates of salads and jars of condiments. Chilo and Shilo retrieved from their eskies tied bundles of cloth that emitted aromas powerful enough to make the native wildlife reconsider their evolutionary dietary choices. Chilo untied his bundle of a selection of dried fish and olives and placed it out for all to share. Shilo similarly unbundled barley bread, figs and feta, all divided into precise portions.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy took a seat nearest the drinks table, under which she\'92d hidden her patches. Hayley was drawn to the seat by Kathy but was momentarily swayed by the stench of Chilo\'92s anchovies, by which time the Chuglingham\'92s  daughter Shyamaladharanithawathie, who had been taking a curious shine to Kathy, slid into the spot. Sarah sat opposite Chip who had positioned himself strategically at the intersection of multiple conversation zones, allowing him to appear engaged while actually participating in nothing. Sarah shot him occasional looks that ranged from encouragement to warning.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91So, Mr. Stockwell,\'92 Padmala said, passing a bowl of Greek salad, \'91Sarah tells us you work with her now? Quite a change from your previous employment, I imagine.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip nodded tersely. \'91Better hours.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I imagine so,\'92 Padmana said, spearing a sausage. \'91Those Fridge Force callouts could happen at any time, couldn't they? Day or night. No warning for anyone involved.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Padmala,\'92 Bala said quietly from farther down the table, \'91perhaps not while we're eating.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Oh, I'm just making conversation,\'92 Paddy smiled, \'91After all, we should know our neighbours, shouldn't we?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Padmala.\'92 Bala's voice carried a warning.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>She set down her fork with deliberate care. \'91I\'92m sorry. We're all just trying to make our way in this strange new world, aren't we? Some of us upward, some of us downward.\'92 She raised her glass. \'91To new beginnings.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>As glasses were raised around the table, the creaking and shutting of the back gate announced a new arrival.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Sorry I'm late,\'92 Eloise called as she advanced on the gathering, wearing hiking gear that appeared to have had never encountered a trail more challenging than a department store escalator. Sarah stood to fetch Eloise a plate and a wine glass while she sat at the rather cramped setting between Chip and Kleo.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Is everyone getting along?\'92 Eloise asked innocently as Shilo poured wine for her. \'91I do hope everyone is being tactful about the past careers of others.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Padmala felt everyone staring at her, even if that wasn\'92t even statistically true.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip never stopped eating.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Bala could not make up his mind whether he should feel honoured or terrified.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91For tax sake, Eloise,\'92 Kathy said from her end of the table, and raised a glass. \'91Take you finger off your button for once, will you?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The silence was only slightly quieter than a nuclear winter.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Is that your medical opinion, Kathy?\'92 Eloise asked icily, then laughed, reached across the table, grabbed Shilo\'92s wine bottle and filled her glass to the brim. \'91I propose a toast,\'92 she said, \'91to people you can actually trust for a change.\'92 She raised her glass a tilted it towards Kathy.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>No one was sure whether they join the salute, and so the table went quiet.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Until Tiki asked, \'91Is this the evil old warlord, Amma?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Padmala reached to enfold her son in protective arms while trying to stop herself from shaking.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Evil old 
\f2\i arms dealer
\f1\i0 , darling,\'92 Eloise said to the boy. \'91Not a warlord. And if you play nice, I might buy you your first gun.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>That only terrified Padmala more.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Later, after the meal, everyone stood to stretch their legs and replenish their drinks. Kleo and Shilo insisted on helping Padmala clean up. Hayley and Kathy finally found moments to themselves, and Eloise made such a discreet exit no one saw her leave.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip retreated to the very back of the yard and found himself savouring the fresh Blue Mountains Air. With his back to the part, he didn\'92t notice Bala Chuglingham approach. The snap of a twig spun Chip around and by then it was too late for Chip to slink away. There was probably a socially acceptable move for it, but Chip lacked the both the method and the skills for it. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The silence between the two men stretched like overtaxed infrastructure. By the time Chip had figured this was just weird and perhaps he could walk away without any explanation, he found himself pinned there by a magisterial stare from Sarah, who stood on the patio trying to disengage from whatever the inebriated Chilo was saying at her.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It\'92s so beautiful here,\'92 Bala finally said, gesturing at the trees with his beer bottle. \'91So \'85 fresh \'85 alive.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip studied his own bottle.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You know, back in the city, under the Layer, we saved for years just to afford a screen that simulated this. A little window of fake sky that cost more than most Cloudborne make in a month. Now my kids see the real thing every day.\'92 He looked at Chip directly. \'91It is good to finally meet you, Mr Stockwell.\'92 Bala's voice had lost its performative cheerfulness.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip exhaled and said nothing.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You understand now, it was out neighbour who broke into our stasis cupboard? Travis Teh, his name was, I believe,\'92 Bala said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'92That\'92s who the police are pinning it on now,\'92 Chip said while pointing at Hayley with his half-empty beer bottle.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91He\'92s dead now, I understand.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91That\'92s how they say it went,\'92 Chip said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It wasn\'92t us, Mr Stockwell,\'92 Bala said, barely above a whisper.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip somehow managed a nod of denial. \'91It was my job. Whenever a stasis unit is compromised, it\'92s always the owners.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Is that what your extensive investigations have uncovered, Mr Stockwell? The kind of investigation you afforded us?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Look at where you are,\'92 Chip said, finding some aggression. \'91I saved you.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Perhaps you did, though not by intent.\'92 Bala, drained the last of his beer and threw the bottle against the fence. He\'92d drank more than he ever should. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Before Chip could respond, Bala\'92s daughter ran up, pointing excitedly at a grey bird with a red head and crest perched in a scribbly gum standing just outside the yard.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Tatta! Tatta! Is that one real too?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Bala's face transformed, the bitterness melting away. "Yes, Shya. That's a real cockatoo. A gang-gang. Not a projection.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The little girl stared in wonder. \'91A gang-gang?\'92 She said, copying her father\'92s word for it. She giggled then dashed off to tell Tiki.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91They sleep better here,\'92 Bala said to Chip. \'91Even with all we lost, they sleep through the night like nothing ever happened.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip wished he slept better\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Funny how things work out,\'92 Bala said. \'91Your lazy, authoritarian, punitive actions put in train a series of events that led directly to where I am today. Where my family is today. I'm in a facilities management for this wondrous place. If you think these townhouses are grand, you should see Eloise\'92s mansion. And the research labs.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I work in the research labs, Mr Chuglingham,\'92 Chip said, finding the fact still rattled him.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Of course, you do,\'92 Bala agreed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip felt a weight in his chest that beer couldn't dissolve. Perhaps it was Kleo\'92s goat or too much honey and feta \'93Mr. Chuglingham, I\'97"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Bala, please." He waved away whatever Chip was about to say. \'91I don't need your excuses, or your rationalisations or your guilt. What would I do with it? Use it to pay for my children's education?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>From the patio, Sarah caught Chip's eye and raised an eyebrow in question. He gave a small nod to indicate he was fine.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I can\'92t say I forgive you, Chip. But neither can I nurture any more animosity toward you, now we are in this place. Let\'92s just agree to be good neighbours. That is all I ask.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>As the evening progressed, the Chuglingham children convinced their father to light the fire pit at the edge of the garden. They gathered around it, faces illuminated by the dancing flames, while Bala told them stories about constellations they could now see without simulation.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip found himself standing apart, watching the family tableau with an unease he couldn't quite name.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91They seem happy,\'92 Sarah said, appearing at his side. \'91Despite everything.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip grunted. \'91I\'92ve stayed longer than I committed.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sensing Chip\'92s imminent departure, Padmala approached. \'91Mr. Stockwell,\'92 she said, \'91it\'92s been enlightening. Do come again. We have so much more to discuss.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Mmm,\'92 wall all Chip could utter as he stared at Sarah, imposing her, 
\f2\i Never let this happen again.
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>As he let the back gate slam behind him, laughter rose from around the fire pit. The Chuglingham children's voices carried in the clear mountain air, delighted by something as simple as marshmallows blackening in flames.
